📡 Multiple Access Protocols Simulation (ALOHA & CSMA)
🎯 Objective
To simulate and evaluate the performance of Pure ALOHA, Slotted ALOHA, CSMA/CD, and CSMA/CA using Cisco Packet Tracer, focusing on collision handling and efficiency.

📘 Protocol Implementation Overview
1. 📡 ALOHA Protocols (Implemented using Hubs)
Pure ALOHA
Nodes transmit whenever data is available. If a collision occurs, they wait a random time and retransmit.

⏱️ Slotted ALOHA
Time is divided into discrete slots. Nodes only transmit at the start of a slot, reducing the collision window by half.

2. 📶 CSMA Protocols (Implemented using Switches/Wireless)
CSMA/CD (Collision Detection)
Nodes listen to the bus before sending. If a collision is detected during transmission, they stop and send a jam signal. (Simulated using Ethernet Half-Duplex)

CSMA/CA (Collision Avoidance)
Used in Wireless LANs. Nodes use a Request to Send / Clear to Send (RTS/CTS) mechanism to reserve the medium.

🖧 Network Topology
Wired Section: Multiple PCs connected to a Central Hub (for ALOHA / CSMA/CD)

Wireless Section: Laptops connected to a Wireless Access Point (for CSMA/CA)

Addressing: Subnet 192.168.1.0/24

🔍 How to Analyze
Open the specific .pkt file for the protocol you wish to test.

Enter Simulation Mode.

Create multiple PDUs (packets) starting at the same time to trigger collisions.

Observe the "Fire" icon in Packet Tracer representing a collision and the subsequent Backoff timers.
